{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli in the Crusader states in the Levant He was born in 1140 and died in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father's death when he was only 12 years old\n\nUnder Raymond III's rule Tripoli faced threats from Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler of Aleppo and Damascus and from Saladin the Ayyubid sultan of Egypt and Syria Raymond III allied with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Knights Templar to defend against these threats\n\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for 11 years He was released in 1175 after the death of Nur ad-Din and the accession of Saladin\n\nUpon his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and continued his efforts to defend the Crusader states He participated in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 and the Battle of Marj Ayyun in 1179 Both battles were victories for the Crusaders against Saladin's forces\n\nRaymond III's rule was marked by conflicts with the Knights Hospitaller and the Knights Templar over issues of power and territory In 1183 he was excommunicated by the Church for his treatment of the Hospitallers\n\nIn 1187 Raymond III joined the forces of Guy of Lusignan the King of Jerusalem for the Battle of Hattin against Saladin's army The Crusader forces were defeated and Raymond III was once again captured by Saladin He was held prisoner until his death in 1187\n\n*Excommunication by the Church*\n\nRaymond III's conflicts with the Knights Hospitaller and the Knights Templar led to his excommunication by the Church in 1183 This was a significant event as it meant that Raymond III was considered to be outside the communion of the Church and was therefore excluded from receiving its sacraments\n\nThe excommunication was the result of Raymond III's treatment of the Hospitallers who had occupied the fortress of Margat in Tripoli without his permission Raymond III demanded that they surrender the fortress to him but they refused He then laid siege to the fortress and when it fell he had the Hospitaller commander hanged\n\nThe Church saw this as an act of aggression against one of its own orders and responded by excommunicating Raymond III The excommunication was lifted after Raymond III's death in 1187\n\n*Battle of Hattin*\n\nThe Battle of Hattin was a decisive victory for Saladin's forces against the Crusader armies in 1187 The battle took place near the Horns of Hattin in present-day Israel and resulted in the capture of thousands of Crusader soldiers including Raymond III\n\nThe defeat at Hattin marked the beginning of the end of the Crusader presence in the Levant and led to the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin's forces later that same year\n\n*Capture and death*\n\nRaymond III was captured by Saladin's forces during the Battle of Hattin and was held prisoner until his death in 1187 The circumstances of his death are not clear but it is believed that he died in captivity\n\nRaymond III's capture and death were significant events as they marked the end of his rule as Count of Tripoli and the beginning of a new era in the Crusader states in the Levant", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -9.107174992095679e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001829695247579366}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.0162993669509888}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -2.1815061700181104e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -8.4638240878121e-06}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.10592442005872726}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.5035098791122437}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.13281051814556122}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -4.0531076592742465e-06}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -0.00010895135346800089}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.19335246